---@class boat_dev.CreateSandboxRequest
---@field org? string Billing wallet for this request: an organization you belong to, by id (`team_…`) or by name as `GET /orgs` lists it (case-insensitive), or `personal`. Omitted, the account's active wallet applies (`PATCH /orgs/active`; personal until set). Two organizations sharing the name answer `409 ambiguous_org` and need the id. Sandboxes and snapshots stay creator-private. On `/environments` routes, only this query parameter selects the organization's shared environments; the `X-Boat-Org` header and the active wallet do not apply there. Members read them, owners and admins change them.
---@field idempotencyKey? string Optional exactly-once key for creating a sandbox. Send your own opaque, account-unique value (a UUID) to make `POST /sandboxes` safe to retry when the response is lost (network timeout, 5xx): the first request creates the sandbox and binds it to the key; every later request with the **same account, key, and request body** returns that same sandbox instead of creating a second, billable one. Behavior: keys are retained for **24 hours**; a concurrent or early retry while the first sandbox is still being minted returns `409` `idempotency_in_progress` (retry shortly, same key); reusing a key with a **different body** returns `409` `idempotency_key_reused`; timeouts and 5xx are safe to retry with the same key; a create that fails before the sandbox exists releases the key within ~2 minutes so a retry can create the sandbox. Omit the header to keep the default (non-idempotent) behavior.
---@field xBoatOrg? string Same as the `org` query parameter. Query wins when both are set.
---@field type? boat_dev.CreateSandboxRequestType|string Machine size. `small` consumes machine time at half rate and `large` at twice the default rate (see the Billing guide). `xlarge` needs the $100/month plan or higher, or an account access grant from an administrator. Otherwise, create, resume and fork return 403 `machine_class_plan_required`. A fork inherits the source sandbox's type unless the fork request passes its own, and resume and fork can move a sandbox between sizes.
---@field ttlSeconds? number|distilled.Null Number of seconds before automatic archival. `null` disables auto-stop. The backend also accepts the string `infinite` for legacy compatibility; new clients should send null.
---@field labels? boat_dev.CreateSandboxRequestLabelsMap Key-value labels, for example your session id. Filter on them with `GET /sandboxes?label=key=value`. At most 20 labels. Keys match `[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._/-]{0,62}`; values are strings of at most 256 bytes.
---@field env? boat_dev.CreateSandboxRequestEnvMap Per-sandbox environment variables injected into the sandbox's tool environment, on top of the account environment's variables (per-sandbox values win on conflicts). Keys must match `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]{0,127}`; at most 100 variables and 64KB total. Reserved names (`ASCII_TOKEN`, `ASCII_API_URL`, `AGENT_ID`, `PRODUCT_MODE`, `ENVIRONMENT_ID`, `BOAT_ID`, `SERVICE_PREVIEW_TOKEN`, `BOAT_CLI_TOKEN`) are rejected with `invalid_env`. Forked sandboxes inherit the source sandbox's env unless the fork request supplies its own `env`.
---@field environment? string Name of the sandbox environment to attach to this sandbox. Environments are managed in the Boat dashboard and bundle the repositories, secrets, and credential toggles a sandbox gets. Omit to use your default environment (`base` unless you changed it). Unknown names are rejected with `unknown_environment`. An environment marked "safe for third parties" passes nothing to the sandbox, exactly like `noEnv`.
---@field noEnv? boolean Create a sandbox with none of the secrets attached to your account (no environment variables, secret files, or credentials), confined to itself so it cannot act on your account or other sandboxes. For sandboxes you give to your own users. SSH, SCP, desktop, snapshots, and public URLs still work; pass `env` to give the sandbox a secret of its own. A fork of a no-env sandbox is always no-env. Equivalent to attaching an environment marked "safe for third parties".
---@field snapshots? boolean Pass `false` to never snapshot this sandbox. Nothing is captured in the background, so it pays no CPU or memory for it, and stopping it erases its disk the way zero data retention does. It cannot be resumed, forked, or saved as a saved snapshot, and if its machine fails there is no backup to rebuild it from.
---@field monitoring? boolean Pass `false` for monitoring off. Boat keeps no command or process output, process list or CPU/RAM samples for this sandbox, and its history, processes and metrics calls answer 409 `monitoring_off`. Live output of a running command still works. While the account default is on, `true` is refused with 409 `monitoring_off_for_account`.
---@field failFast? boolean Pass `true` to get a sandbox only if a machine of this type is ready right now. The call answers within about 1.5 s: either the usual `202` with the sandbox, or `503` `no_ready_machine` with nothing created, billed, or counted against your start limits, so you can retry or fall back at once. Without it, a create waits for a machine to be built when none is ready. Works with `from`; fork and resume take it too.
---@field count? number With `from`: create this many sandboxes from the saved snapshot in one request. Boat creates them together, which is faster than separate creates when you need many sandboxes at once. Start limits and the active-sandbox limit count every sandbox of the batch; if the batch does not fit, nothing is created. The answer lists all of them in `sandboxes` (and the first one in `sandbox`). Refused with 400 `count_requires_from` without `from`, and with 400 `count_with_fail_fast` together with `failFast`.
---@field setupScript? string Shell script that runs on the sandbox after it is ready. Ready means "ready to accept the user", not "setup done": the script starts in the background once provisioning completes and never blocks the sandbox becoming usable. It runs as the sandbox user (`user`, not root; `sudo` needs no password) via `bash`, with the sandbox's environment applied, and its output goes to a log file on the sandbox. Observe the outcome as `setupStatus` (pending/running/done/failed) and `setupError` on the sandbox. Rejected with a 400 `invalid_setup_script` error when it is not a string or exceeds 64KB. When the request also has `files`, the script starts after every file is written.
---@field name? string Display name of the sandbox, the same value `PATCH /sandboxes/{id}` sets. A sandbox created `from` a saved snapshot is named after the snapshot when this is omitted.
---@field visibility? boat_dev.CreateSandboxRequestVisibility|string Who can use a sandbox billed to an organization, with the same rules as the sandbox's `visibility` field. `private`: only you can use it. `shared`: every member can, as long as it holds none of your personal logins (`noEnv`, or an environment that passes nothing). Omitted, the organization's default for new sandboxes applies (`PATCH /orgs/{org}` `newSandboxesPrivate`). It is set before the sandbox gets a machine, so a `private` sandbox is never shared, not even for a moment, and needs no `unshare` call. A personal sandbox is always private: `shared` is refused with 400 `not_org_sandbox`. Change it later with `POST /sandboxes/{id}/share` and `POST /sandboxes/{id}/unshare`.
---@field hostedPorts? boat_dev.CreateSandboxRequestHostedPortsList Ports to host on public HTTPS URLs, each exactly as `POST /sandboxes/{id}/host` does. The create waits for the sandbox's machine (up to 60 s, usually under a second), hosts every port at once and answers the URLs in `hostedPorts`. The ports are routed before the sandbox is ready, so a service that starts later is reachable as soon as it listens. Refused with 400 `invalid_hosted_ports` for a bad entry or a duplicate port, with 400 `count_with_hosted_ports` together with `count`, and with 400 `fail_fast_with_hosted_ports` together with `failFast`.
---@field files? boat_dev.CreateSandboxRequestFilesList Files to write on the sandbox, each like `PUT /sandboxes/{id}/files` (a path under `/home/user` or `/tmp`, relative paths resolve against `/home/user`; missing folders are created). Boat writes them once the sandbox's disk is in place and before it reports `ready`, so a client that sees `ready`, and the `setupScript`, always find them. They are written once, at the first start: a resume does not write them again. At most 256KB in total; upload bigger files with `POST /sandboxes/{id}/files/upload-url`. `setupStatus` covers them: it stays `pending` until they are written, and a write that fails sets it `failed` with the path and the reason in `setupError` (the setup script then does not run). Refused with 400 `invalid_files` for a bad path, a duplicate path or a body over the limits.
---@field teamId? string Legacy alias for `org`. Ignored when `org` is also set.
---@field from? string Create the sandbox from a saved snapshot (saved with `POST /saved-snapshots`, or `boat snapshot <id> <name>` in the CLI). The sandbox starts from that exact frozen state. Omitting `type` inherits the type the snapshot was saved from; env and no-env inherit from the snapshot's source sandbox unless the request passes its own, with the same rules as forking. Also accepts a share code (`bsh_...`) that another account made with `POST /saved-snapshots/{name}/shares`. The new sandbox lives in and is billed to your account. It does not get the owner's per-sandbox env; your own environment applies normally. A wrong or revoked code returns `404 snapshot_not_found`.

